"The postoperative pathology revealed a Gleason 3+3 with tertiary pattern 4 (ISUP grade group 1) and pathological stage pT2c. A positive surgical margin was documented, which is an adverse finding. Countervailing factors include the absence of extraprostatic extension and no invasion of the seminal vesicles, as stated by the surgical pathology report: \"There was no extraprostatic extension; surgical margins were positive; the seminal vesicles were not invaded; lymphovascular invasion was absent; no lymph nodes were removed.\" The nodal status is pNx, meaning no nodes were removed, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal involvement. Biopsy results regarding Gleason pattern and ISUP grade are missing from the pathology report. Postoperative PSA is not recorded, meaning biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its trajectory is crucial for revising the prognosis. The current findings suggest a risk profile influenced by the positive margins, tempered by the absence of local extension and seminal vesicle invasion. The fixed estimated horizon is 70.80 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 42.48–99.12 months; its coverage has not been validated."
